"HBM became an industry standard in 2013, and it has grown quickly since. Each new generation sends data faster through every lane, and the towers get taller: 4 floors, then 8, 12, and 16. The newest standard, HBM4, doubles the highway to 2,048 lanes. One HBM4 stack can move about 2,048 gigabytes in a single second.": "a9758a99673d",
"HBM was made to fix this. The letters stand for High Bandwidth Memory. Bandwidth means how much data can move in one second. Engineers did three things. First, they stacked memory chips on top of one another, like the floors of a tower. Second, they ran tiny copper posts, called TSVs, straight through the stack, like elevators, so every floor is connected.": "f7c4ac429274",
"Imagine the best cook in the world working in a kitchen where the refrigerator is at the other end of the street. The cook is fast, but most of the time is spent walking to fetch the eggs. A chip that runs AI has the same trouble. It can do an enormous number of calculations each second, but only if the numbers it needs arrive in time.": "277d82a2492e",
"Imagine the best cook in the world working in a kitchen where the refrigerator is at the other end of the street. The cook is fast, but most of the time is spent walking to fetch the eggs. A chip that runs AI has the same trouble. It can do an enormous number of calculations each second, but only if the numbers it needs arrive in time. Those numbers are kept in memory chips. In an ordinary computer, the memory sits in its own packages some distance from the logic chip, and each memory chip talks through only a few dozen data lines. For everyday work that is fine. For AI, which moves mountains of data, the logic chip ends up waiting. The memory cannot deliver fast enough. HBM was made to fix this. The letters stand for High Bandwidth Memory. Bandwidth means how much data can move in one second. Engineers did three things. First, they stacked memory chips on top of one another, like the floors of a tower. Second, they ran tiny copper posts, called TSVs, straight through the stack, like elevators, so every floor is connected. Third, they moved the tower right next to the logic chip, on the same interposer you met in the last lesson. Because the two are so close, they can be joined by a huge number of very short wires. The first HBM had 1,024 data lines for each stack. Think of a highway with more than a thousand lanes that is very, very short. HBM became an industry standard in 2013, and it has grown quickly since. Each new generation sends data faster through every lane, and the towers get taller: 4 floors, then 8, 12, and 16. The newest standard, HBM4, doubles the highway to 2,048 lanes. One HBM4 stack can move about 2,048 gigabytes in a single second. That is why you now hear about HBM in the news. An AI chip usually has several HBM towers standing around it, feeding it data all day. The memory is no longer far down the street. The refrigerator has moved right beside the cook, and it has a thousand doors.": "937b3cfdc8dc",
